## What's happening

`PUT /user` writes the new password into the database **in plaintext**, and then login can never match it.

The chain:

1. **`src/user/user.controller.ts:26-29`** — `PUT /user` has no `@UsePipes(new ValidationPipe())` (unlike `create` and `login` right below it). Whatever is in `body.user` reaches the service.
2. **`src/user/user.service.ts:74-81`** — `update()` does `delete toUpdate.password` (good intent) and then immediately `Object.assign(toUpdate, dto)`, which puts the client's `password` straight back. `UpdateUserDto` declares no `password` field, but that's a TypeScript annotation — it strips nothing at runtime.
3. **`src/user/user.entity.ts:28-31`** — `hashPassword()` is `@BeforeInsert()` **only**. There is no `@BeforeUpdate()`. Since the entity has an `id`, `save()` issues an UPDATE, so the hook never fires.
4. The plaintext string lands in the `password` column.
5. **`src/user/user.service.ts:31`** — login calls `argon2.verify(user.password, password)` where `user.password` is now `"newpassword"`, not a `$argon2id$...` digest. It can never succeed.

So the user's account isn't locked out by some session/token issue — their stored credential is structurally invalid.

This is a known incident on this codebase: it's written up in `LESSONS.md:7-15` (upstream issue #88) and flagged in `.claude/knowledge/schema.md:20`. The lesson was recorded but the code was never fixed.

**One detail I could not confirm empirically:** `node_modules` isn't installed here, so I couldn't run it. With `argon2@^0.26.2`, `verify()` deserializes the digest via `@phc/format`, which *throws* on a string with no `$` rather than returning `false`. If so, affected users get a **500**, not the `401 {errors: {User: ' not found'}}` the controller intends — worth checking against your actual error logs, since it changes what the client shows them.

## The fix

Hash explicitly in the update path, rather than adding `@BeforeUpdate()` to the entity. A `@BeforeUpdate()` hook would fire on *every* save of an existing user — changing only a bio would re-hash the already-hashed value and break login a second way, and it would hit `argon2.hash(undefined)` whenever no password is sent.

```ts
async update(id: number, dto: UpdateUserDto): Promise<UserEntity> {
  const toUpdate = await this.userRepository.findOne(id);

  // copy allowed fields by name — the DTO class is not a runtime filter
  if (dto.username !== undefined) toUpdate.username = dto.username;
  if (dto.email !== undefined) toUpdate.email = dto.email;
  if (dto.bio !== undefined) toUpdate.bio = dto.bio;
  if (dto.image !== undefined) toUpdate.image = dto.image;

  const password = (dto as any).password;
  if (password) {
    toUpdate.password = await argon2.hash(password);
  }

  return await this.userRepository.save(toUpdate);
}
```

If you want password change supported as a real feature, add `password` to `UpdateUserDto` and keep the explicit `argon2.hash`. The `Object.assign`-the-body pattern is the same one `LESSONS.md:39-47` warns about, and `ArticleService.update()` has it too.

## Don't forget the already-corrupted rows

Every user who changed their password since this shipped has their **actual plaintext password sitting in MySQL**. Two things:

- **Recovery is possible** — the stored value *is* the password they chose, so hashing it in place restores their login: for each row where `password NOT LIKE '$argon2%'`, set `password = argon2.hash(password)`. Run it as a one-off script (argon2 hashing can't be done in SQL).
- **Treat it as a credential exposure** regardless. Those passwords were at rest in the clear and are likely in DB backups and possibly query logs. Standard handling is to force a reset and notify affected users rather than silently re-hash.

